Part A: Iterative loops and Compiler optimizations.
 
 :For loop N(100,000,000) number of times
 :Each iteration of the loop takes around 1 nanosecond
 :Hence, 100,000,000 repetitions takes a fraction of a second.
 
 :Using different compiler optimizations (GCC)
 : -O0 -> no optimization (the slowest)

        -O0 is slow because every single loop step forces heavy read/write 
        traffic back to slow RAM.

        Every variable is continuously read from and written
        back to slow RAM/stack memory.

 : -O1 -> basic Optimization

        -O1 speeds up by pulling those loop variables out of RAM 
        and keeping them right inside CPU registers.

        Moves frequently used loop counters and variables out 
        of slow RAM and stores them inside fast CPU registers. 
        Removes dead/unused code.
 
 : -O2 -> Production Standard Optimization

        -O2 optimizes pipeline flow by inlining functions 
        and eliminating redundant memory loads.

        Inlines simple function calls directly, reorders machine
        instructions to keep the CPU execution pipeline full, and
        eliminates redundant memory loads.

 : -O3 -> Aggressive Optimization

        -O3 is maximum speed because GCC unrolls the loop body and 
        uses SIMD vectorization to execute multiple additions in a 
        single CPU clock cycle."*
        
        Performs aggressive loop unrolling 
        (duplicates loop bodies to eliminate loop exit checks) 
        and SIMD vectorization 
        (uses Single Instruction, Multiple Data registers 
        to execute multiple additions per clock cycle).





PART B: recursive vs iterative
 : comparing the timings beween 
 : recursive functions and iterative loops
 : 
 : Iterative loops
    reuse the exact memory space and stack frame over and over.
 :
 : Recursive functions
    allocate new stack frame on the CPU for every single recursion.
 :
 : N=10,000 instead of 100,000,000
    100 million stack frames will be created at once,
    instantly exhauting memory and crashing the program with a 
    segmentation fault (Stack overflow).

    We capped recursion at $N = 10,000$ because passing 100 million 
    would create 100 million stack frames at once, exceeding stack 
    memory limits and causing a Stack Overflow crash. 
    Iteration reuses a single stack frame, whereas recursion 
    allocates a new frame for every call.


why volatile:
Declaring a variable as volatile explicitly forces the compiler
to treat every read and write to that variable as an actual, 
unskippable hardware memory operation. 

This prevents GCC from optimizing the execution t
time down to 0ns so you can measure 
true benchmark speedups.